REM dhancha — realise one port of an existing interface
CYCLER "port"
TITLE "Bring up one port"
OUTPUT KIND PORT
RESOLUTION MID

STAGE CONCEPT
  VERB CREATE
  SHAPE TEXT
  ASK <<
    Which substrate, and what is physically present when this port runs?
    Name the hardware or runtime by part number or version. "An embedded board" is
    not an answer. If nothing is present and this is a simulation, say so here —
    a simulated bring-up reported as a bring-up is the defect this stage exists to stop.
  >>
  EXPECT TEXT
END STAGE

STAGE ENVELOPE
  VERB MEASURE
  SHAPE JSON
  ASK <<
    State the operating envelope of this substrate: the limits, what happens outside
    them, and how it fails. Unknown limits are written: not established.
  >>
  EXPECT JSON
END STAGE

STAGE IMPLEMENT
  VERB CREATE
  SHAPE TEXT
  ASK <<
    Implement every function in the interface for this substrate. An operation the
    substrate cannot perform returns the unsupported code — it never returns success
    with a fabricated value.
  >>
  EXPECT TEXT
END STAGE

STAGE HARNESS
  VERB EXECUTE
  SHAPE TEXT
  ASK <<
    Run the conformance harness against this port. Paste the output.
    A condition marked not established cannot pass and cannot fail; it is reported
    as unjudged.
  >>
  EXPECT TEXT
END STAGE

STAGE EVIDENCE
  VERB MEASURE
  SHAPE DATA
  ASK <<
    Attach the captured evidence: the transcript, the trace, the log, timestamped.
    Evidence is captured, never generated. If you have none, say you have none.
  >>
  EXPECT DATA
END STAGE

STAGE GATE
  VERB VERIFY
  SHAPE TEXT
  ASK <<
    State what is proven on real hardware, what is proven only against the mock, and
    what is not proven at all. Three lists. An empty list is a valid answer.
  >>
  EXPECT TEXT
  GATE "PORT ready"
END STAGE
